1135	1138	1140	1141	the doctor	-1	“ Well , ”
1160	1201	1140	1141	the doctor	-1	“ I hope you ’ll be easy , Mis . Hall , now that we ’ve toted out here , bag and baggage , to please you , when I supposed I was settled for the rest of my life . ”
1202	1225	1227	1229	the old lady	-1	“ Fathers ca n’t be expected to have as much natural affection , or to be as self - sacrificing as mothers , ”
1231	1291	1227	1229	the old lady	-1	“ Of course , it was some trouble to move out here ; but , for Harry ’s sake , I was willing to do it . What does Ruth know about house - keeping , I ’d like to know ? A pretty muss she ’ll make of it , if I ’m not around to oversee things . ”
1292	1297	1299	1300	the doctor	-1	“ It strikes me , ”
1302	1354	1299	1300	the doctor	-1	“ that you wo n’t get any thanks for it — from one side of the house , at least . Ruth never says anything when you vex her , but there ’s a look in her eye which — well , Mis . Hall , it tells the whole story . ”
1358	1364	1366	1368	the old lady	-1	“ I ’ve seen it , ”
1380	1533	1366	1368	the old lady	-1	“ and it has provoked me a thousand times more than if she had thrown a brick - bat at my head . That girl is no fool , doctor . She knows very well what she is about : but diamond cut diamond , I say . Doctor , doctor , there are the hens in the garden . I want that garden kept nice . I suppose Ruth thinks that nobody can have flowers but herself . Wait till my china - asters and sweet peas come up . I ’m going over to - day to take a peep round her house ; I wonder what it looks like ? Stuck full of gimcracks , of all sorts , I ’ll warrant . Well , I sha n’t furnish my best parlor till I see what she has got . I ’ve laid by a little money , and — ”
1534	1546	1548	1549	the doctor	-1	“ Better give it to the missionaries , Mis . Hall , ”
1551	1568	1548	1549	the doctor	-1	“ I tell you Ruth do n’t care a pin what you have in your parlor . ”
1569	1576	1578	1580	the old lady	-1	“ Do n’t you believe it , ”
1582	1587	1589	1590	the doctor	-1	“ Well , anyhow , ”
1592	1664	1589	1590	the doctor	-1	“ you ca n’t get the upper hand of her in that line ; i. e. , if she has a mind that you shall not . Harry is doing a very good business ; and you know very well , it is no use to try to blind your eyes to it , that if she wanted Queen Victoria ’s sceptre , he ’d manage to get it for her . ”
1665	1676	1678	1680	the old lady	-1	“ That ’s more than I can say of you , ”
1686	1775	1777	1779	the old lady	-1	“ for all that I used to mend your old saddle - bags , and once made , with my[54 ] own hands , a pair of leather small - clothes to ride horseback in . Forty years , doctor , I ’ve spent in your service . I do n’t look much as I did when you married me . I was said then to have ‘ woman ’s seven beauties , ’ including the ‘ dimple in the chin , ’ which I see still remains ; ”
1790	1834	1777	1779	the old lady	-1	“ I might have had him that was Squire Smith , or Pete Packer , or Jim Jessup . There was n’t one of ’em who had not rather do the chores on our farm , than on any other in the village . ”
